  "worked_solution": "First, we rearrange the equation $y-2x=-11,$ as follows: \n\n$$\ny=2x-11\n$$\n\nTo find $x$-coordinate of the intersection point, we need to substitute $y=2x-11$ into the equation of the circle and solve the resulting equation for $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x-3)^{2}+{y}^{2} & =5 \\\\ (x-3)^{2}+(2x-11)^{2} & =5 \\\\ {x}^{2}-6x+9+4{x}^{2}-44x+121 & =5 \\\\ 5{x}^{2}-50x+130 & =5 \\\\ 5{x}^{2}-50x+125 & =0 \\\\ {x}^{2}-10x+25 & =0 \\\\ (x-5)^{2} & =0 \\\\ x-5 & =0 \\\\ x & =5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find $y$-coordinate of the intersection point, we need to substitute $x=5$ into the equation of the line and compute $y$-value, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =2x-11 \\\\  & =2(5)-11 \\\\  & =-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the line $y-2x=-11$ intersects the circle $(x-3)^{2}+{y}^{2}=5$ at the point $(5,-1).$",
